Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers from a power meter
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus, pyModbusTCP
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, 
    │   result.isError, result.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus's read functions raise exceptions on error 
    │   instead of returning an object with isError(), so error-handling logic 
    │   must be rewritten; minimalmodbus returns raw list of register values 
    │   directly, not a response object with a .registers attribute; 
    │   pyModbusTCP's read_holding_registers returns None on failure rather 
    │   than an object with isError(), requiring different failure detection 
    │   logic; Unit/slave ID handling differs across libraries (pymodbus uses 
    │   'unit=' kwarg historically, newer versions use 'slave='; minimalmodbus 
    │   sets slaveaddress in the constructor); Connection lifecycle methods 
    │   differ: pymodbus needs explicit .connect(), minimalmodbus/pyModbusTCP 
    │   handle connections differently
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus master/client functionality capable 
        of reading holding registers over TCP, making them functionally 
        interchangeable at a high level. pymodbus is the incumbent (host/port 
        constructor, connect(), read_holding_registers returning a response 
        object with .isError()/.registers). minimalmodbus is explicitly called 
        out in the file's own docstring as the intended substitution target, 
        though it normally returns raw register lists and raises exceptions 
        instead of using isError() -- requiring wrapper/glue code. 
        pyModbusTCP's ModbusClient offers a similar host/port TCP client model 
        with a read_holding_registers() call returning a list or None, which is
        a plausible but non-identical substitute.

