└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, umodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, 
    │   client.read_holding_registers, result.isError, result.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RTU) 
    │   communication; TCP usage is less idiomatic and requires different 
    │   setup; pymodbus returns a response object with `.registers` and 
    │   `.isError()`; minimalmodbus's `read_registers` returns a raw list and 
    │   raises exceptions instead of an error flag; pyModbusTCP uses 
    │   `ModbusClient.read_holding_registers` returning a list or None on 
    │   failure, not an object with `.isError()`; modbus-tk uses a different 
    │   API pattern (`master.execute` with function codes) rather than named 
    │   register-read methods; umodbus is primarily designed for building 
    │   Modbus servers, not clients, so its client API is limited and less 
    │   mature; unit/slave ID parameter naming and defaults differ across 
    │   libraries (`unit=` vs `slaveaddress=` vs `unit_id=`)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── pymodbus is used here as a TCP Modbus client to connect to a meter and 
        read holding registers, returning a response object with `.registers` 
        and `.isError()`. minimalmodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, and umodbus 
        are all libraries that implement Modbus TCP/RTU communication and could
        functionally replace pymodbus for reading holding registers from a 
        device. However, their client construction, connection handling, and 
        result representations differ significantly.

