    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns a response object with .isError() and 
    │   .registers; minimalmodbus returns raw register lists directly and 
    │   raises exceptions on error instead of an isError() flag; pyModbusTCP's 
    │   ModbusClient.read_holding_registers returns a plain list or None on 
    │   failure, not an object with isError(); minimalmodbus is fundamentally 
    │   serial-oriented (RS232/RS485); using it over TCP requires additional 
    │   shims or a different transport layer, unlike pymodbus/pyModbusTCP which
    │   are TCP-native; Unit/slave ID parameter naming and defaults differ 
    │   across libraries (e.g., 'unit' vs 'slaveaddress'); Connection lifecycle
    │   methods differ (connect()/close() naming and behavior)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus master/client functionality capable 
        of reading holding registers from a remote device, which is the sole 
        external I/O role in this file (via 
        ModbusTcpClient.read_holding_registers). pyModbusTCP is TCP-native like
        pymodbus and exposes a similar client object model. minimalmodbus is 
        explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as the intended 
        substitution target, though it is primarily designed for serial (RTU) 
        communication and requires extra setup/wrapping to work over TCP.

