└── finite-state machine engine for modeling transitions with guards/callbacks
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, automat, pysm
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, model binding (self.state), 
    │   conditions=, after=
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions' model=self dynamic-injection pattern (methods
    │   like self.start_fill(), self.state) has no direct equivalent in 
    │   python-statemachine, which requires class-level State/Transition 
    │   descriptors defined on the StateMachine subclass itself rather than 
    │   injected into an external model object.; automat uses a fully 
    │   declarative decorator-based API (@MethodicalMachine.state, @.input, 
    │   @.output) that requires restructuring guard/callback methods into 
    │   decorated inputs/outputs — not a drop-in replacement.; pysm has a 
    │   different transition/event API (Event objects, State.handlers) and does
    │   not support the 'conditions'/'after' string-callback resolution used 
    │   here.; Wildcard transitions ('*' source state used in trip()) are a 
    │   transitions-specific feature not uniformly supported across 
    │   alternatives.; Migrating would require restructuring TankController's 
    │   class definition and callback wiring, not just swapping the import 
    │   line.
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions is used here to build a dynamic state machine bound to an 
        arbitrary Python object (model=self), injecting transition trigger 
        methods (start_fill, level_reached, etc.) at runtime based on 
        string-named conditions/callbacks. python-statemachine, automat, and 
        pysm are all general-purpose FSM libraries that could theoretically 
        replace transitions for expressing states/transitions/guards, making 
        them functionally in the same category (declarative/programmatic FSM 
        engines for Python).

