    │   python-statemachine and automat instead require states/transitions to 
    │   be declared as class attributes/decorators, requiring a full rewrite of
    │   TankController's class body, not just import swaps; wildcard source 
    │   state '*' (used in 'trip' transition) is a transitions-specific 
    │   convenience; equivalent behavior must be manually reproduced (e.g., 
    │   listing all states) in python-statemachine/automat; the 'conditions' 
    │   and 'after' string-based callback binding (looked up by name on the 
    │   model) differs from automat's decorator-based observer callbacks and 
    │   python-statemachine's on_<event>/before_<event> hook naming convention;
    │   self.state attribute access pattern differs across libraries 
    │   (python-statemachine uses a StateField descriptor, automat has no 
    │   direct '.state' attribute by default)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions is used here as a generic FSM engine that dynamically 
        attaches trigger methods to the model object and manages state 
        transitions with conditions/after callbacks and wildcard transitions. 
        python-statemachine and automat serve the same functional role 
        (declarative/generic state machine management for Python objects) and 
        could in principle replace transitions, but their APIs are structurally
        different (class-based State/Event declarations for 
        python-statemachine, decorator-based MethodicalMachine for automat) 
        rather than the dynamic Machine(model=...) pattern used here.

