│   │   write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
│   ├── Pitfalls   : pyModbusTCP uses a different API: 
│   │   ModbusClient.read_holding_registers returns a list or None on failure 
│   │   instead of a response object with isError(); modbus-tk uses a 
│   │   Master/RtuMaster or TcpMaster with execute() calls and a different 
│   │   exception-based error model instead of isError() checks; unit/slave id 
│   │   parameter naming and position differs across libraries (unit= vs 
│   │   slave_id vs unit_id); Register addressing and read/write function code 
│   │   selection conventions may differ slightly between libraries; Connection
│   │   lifecycle management (connect/close, context managers) differs across 
│   │   libraries
│   ├── Constraints: requires_connector
│   └── All three libraries provide Modbus TCP master/client functionality 
│       capable of reading and writing holding registers over TCP to a 
│       slave/unit device, matching the file's actual usage (ModbusTcpClient 
│       with host/port, read_holding_registers, write_register, unit id). 
│       pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk are commonly used drop-in-ish alternatives to
│       pymodbus for TCP-based Modbus communication.
└── Modbus client abstraction with fundamentally different transport (excluded 
    from primary swap due to serial-only limitation)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, read_holding_registers, write_register
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus has no native TCP transport; it targets 
    │   serial ports (COM/tty) requiring pyserial and a serial-to-TCP gateway 
    │   to reach the same TCP device; minimalmodbus's 
    │   Instrument.read_register/write_register uses positional decimal-place 
    │   and functioncode arguments rather than isError() response checking; No 
    │   direct equivalent of ModbusTcpClient(host, port=...) constructor; 
    │   connection model is fundamentally different; Error handling raises 
    │   exceptions (e.g., ModbusException) rather than returning an object with
    │   isError()
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file's own docstring explicitly calls out pymodbus -> minimalmodbus
        as a substitution target, but minimalmodbus is designed for serial 
        (RTU/ASCII) Modbus communication via pyserial, not native TCP sockets, 
        and exposes a completely different Instrument-based 
        read_register/write_register API with a distinct error model, so it is 
        only a viable substitute with significant glue code.

