Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus (industrial fieldbus) client library for reading/writing holding 
    registers over TCP
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, 
    │   write_register, isError, registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus's read_holding_registers/write_register accept a 
    │   'unit' keyword and return response objects with .isError()/.registers; 
    │   pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk use different call signatures (e.g., 
    │   read_holding_registers(reg, nb) returning a list or None on failure) 
    │   and lack a unified error object with .isError(); minimalmodbus is 
    │   fundamentally serial-RTU oriented; using it for TCP transport requires 
    │   additional handling and its Instrument.read_register/write_register API
    │   and exception-based error model (raises on failure vs. isError() check)
    │   requires rewriting ModbusIO's error handling; Slave/unit addressing 
    │   conventions differ across libraries (unit= vs slaveaddress= vs implicit
    │   in TcpMaster.execute), so address/unit-id semantics must be 
    │   re-validated; Connection lifecycle differs: pymodbus needs explicit 
    │   .connect(), pyModbusTCP auto-opens on first request, modbus-tk requires
    │   explicit TcpMaster + open()
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide client-side Modbus protocol support for 
        polling/writing registers on industrial devices (boilers, PLCs, etc.), 
        which is the exact role pymodbus plays here 
        (ModbusTcpClient.connect/read_holding_registers/write_register). 
        pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk are TCP-native like pymodbus and could serve 
        as direct substitutes for the ModbusIO wrapper. minimalmodbus is 
        explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a substitution 
        target, though it is primarily a serial/RTU library (its TCP support is
        limited/newer) and has a materially different API and error-handling 
        model.

