    │   .registers; pyModbusTCP's read_holding_registers returns a plain list 
    │   or None on failure, requiring different error-handling logic; 
    │   minimalmodbus is designed around serial/RTU communication with a 
    │   synchronous register-based API (read_register/write_register) lacking 
    │   the isError() pattern -- explicit glue code required as noted in the 
    │   docstring; modbus-tk has a very different API structure (master/query 
    │   pattern) requiring a rewrite of read/write calls; umodbus is primarily 
    │   designed for building Modbus servers, not client-side polling, making 
    │   it a poor fit despite protocol overlap; The `unit=` keyword argument 
    │   for slave/unit id differs in name and semantics across these libraries 
    │   (e.g., slave_id, unit, address)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All are Python libraries implementing the Modbus protocol for talking 
        to PLCs/field devices over TCP or serial, and could in principle 
        replace pymodbus as the transport layer in this file. pyModbusTCP is 
        the closest functional match (pure TCP client), while modbus-tk and 
        umodbus support both client/server roles, and minimalmodbus is 
        primarily RTU/serial-oriented (explicitly called out in the file's own 
        docstring as needing a different read/write API and error model).

