Equivalence Groups Found
└── synchronous HTTP client (Session-based) for SCADA REST API calls
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, session.post, raise_for_status, 
    │   json, status_code
    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client vs requests.Session: exception hierarchies 
    │   differ (httpx.HTTPStatusError vs requests.HTTPError) — 
    │   raise_for_status() behavior/exception types must be reconciled; Timeout
    │   semantics differ: httpx timeouts are more granular 
    │   (connect/read/write/pool) vs requests' single float timeout; httpx does
    │   not automatically follow redirects on POST by default in some versions 
    │   (follow_redirects flag), differing from requests' default behavior; 
    │   Response object differences: httpx.Response.json() and .status_code are
    │   compatible but subtle behavior (e.g., encoding detection) can differ
    └── The code uses a requests.Session object injected into ScadaClient, 
        calling .get()/.post() with timeout, .raise_for_status(), and .json() 
        on the response. httpx.Client exposes a nearly identical synchronous 
        API (Client.get/post, raise_for_status, .json()) and can serve as a 
        drop-in sync replacement, which the file's own docstring explicitly 
        calls out as a substitution target. urllib3 is excluded because 
        requests is built directly on top of urllib3 (dependency chain rule 
        violation, not a peer alternative). aiohttp is excluded because it is 
        fundamentally async (async/await, ClientSession context managers) and 
        using it would require rewriting get_demand/get_runtimes/command into 
        coroutines — a structural rewrite beyond simple import/call 
        substitution, not a like-for-like session/client swap.

